    "result_text": "Build succeeds and the test passes.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `bulkSignupWarning` flag as a derived, read-time field on the owner response.\n\n**Design:** The flag reuses the same per-day registration count the daily-limit rule already relies on (`ClinicService.countOwnersRegisteredOn`), just with a lower threshold \u2014 so it's the same accumulation path, per the test's own note.\n\nChanges, each in its own single-responsibility unit:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a `readOnly` boolean `bulkSignupWarning` to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` gains the field.\n\n2. **`service/BulkSignupWarningEvaluator.java`** (new) \u2014 a small component that answers one question: is the warning warranted for a given owner? It returns `true` when more than 80 owners (`BULK_SIGNUP_THRESHOLD`) share the owner's registration date, `false` otherwise (including when the owner/date isn't known). It sits beside the count logic in the service layer, delegating to `ClinicService` rather than duplicating the count.\n\n3. **`mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 converted the interface to an abstract class (component model is already `spring`) so it can hold the injected evaluator, then added `bulkSignupWarning` as a mapped expression alongside the existing derived fields (`displayName`, `initials`, etc.). This keeps the warning consistent across every owner response (get, list, create) via the one place that builds `OwnerDto`, rather than scattering it across controller methods.\n\nThe `AcceptanceTest` (fresh create \u2192 `bulkSignupWarning: false`) passes, and the full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test, 0 failures).",
